<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oh, the Red Viburnum in the Meadow</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">rooted in a song of the mid-17th century; recorded <a href="/years/1875">1875</a>; march of <a href="/years/1914">1914</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Ой, у лузі червона калина, Oi u luzi chervona kalyna</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Oi u luzi chervona kalyna&quot; (&quot;Oh, the Red Viburnum in the Meadow&quot;) is a Ukrainian patriotic march. Its text derives from a folk song, &quot;The Steep Banks Overflowed&quot;, which dates from the Khmelnytskyi Uprising of the mid-17th century.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The text of the song derives from a folk composition, The Steep Banks Overflowed, dating from the Khmelnytskyi Uprising. The most popular variant was recorded in 1875 by the historians Volodymyr Antonovych and Mykhailo Drahomanov in the village of Marianivka, then part of Yelysavethrad county in the Kherson Governorate.</p><p>It became widely known through Stepan Charnetskyi's performance Sun of the Ruin, based on a play by Vasyl Pachovsky, which premiered in early 1914 and was shown at the Ruska Besida theatre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lviv</a> and around Eastern Galicia. The conductor Mykhailo Kossak arranged the melody. In September 1914 Hryhoriy Trukh, an officer of the Ukrainian Sich Riflemen, heard the song in Stryi and wrote three new strophes for it, and it became the unit's anthem.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The red viburnum, kalyna, is a national symbol of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. In Ukrainian culture its berries symbolise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> blood spilled in battle, and in many ballads they are a metaphor for untimely death and are believed to grow where young men were buried.</p><p>Between the wars the song was arranged for choir by Alexander Koshetz and Filaret Kolessa and spread around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, no longer tied only to the military. In 1925 the singer Mykhailo Zozuliak recorded it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. The Ukrainian Insurgent Army also used it during and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. Singing it was banned while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> was a Soviet republic, from 1919 to 1991, and it was passed on mostly in family circles and among dissidents, although a version of the text was published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a> in 1961. Open performances became widespread in the late 1980s at protest meetings.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In March 2022 an Instagram video of an a cappella rendition of the first verse by Andriy Khlyvnyuk of the band BoomBox drew international attention. He recorded it in army fatigues near Sophia Square in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a> after returning to join the armed forces, and posted it on 27 February, three days after the Russian invasion began. Artists around the world remixed it, and in April 2022 Pink Floyd used his voice in the single Hey, Hey, Rise Up! to raise money for Ukrainian humanitarian relief. On 28 June 2022 more than 1,000 singers from over 50 countries performed it in what was recorded as the world's largest online performance.</p><p>After the annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> in 2014 and the invasion of 2022, Russian authorities in Crimea treated singing it as discrediting the Russian army and punished it with fines and imprisonment.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oi u luzi chervona kalyna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;Oi u luzi chervona kalyna&quot; (&quot;<b>Oh, the Red Viburnum in the Meadow</b>&quot;) is a Ukrainian patriotic march.</blockquote><a href="/songs/oi-u-luzi-chervona-kalyna">Oh, the Red Viburnum in the Meadow</a></li></ul></section>
